The problem involves calculating the time it takes for Tom to return on his ship, given his speeds and the duration of his initial trip.

---

### Step 1: Determine the duration of the initial trip
Tom starts at **1 PM** and finishes at **4 PM**, so the time he spends sailing **out** is:
$$
4\, \text{PM} - 1\, \text{PM} = 3\, \text{hours}
$$

---

### Step 2: Calculate the distance traveled
Tom sails at a speed of **10 mph** during this time. Using the formula:
$$
\text{Distance} = \text{Speed} \times \text{Time}
$$
$$
\text{Distance} = 10\, \text{mph} \times 3\, \text{hours} = 30\, \text{miles}
$$

So, the distance between the starting point and the destination is **30 miles**.

---

### Step 3: Calculate the time to return
On the way back, Tom sails at a speed of **6 mph**. The time required to travel back is:
$$
\text{Time} = \frac{\text{Distance}}{\text{Speed}} = \frac{30\, \text{miles}}{6\, \text{mph}} = 5\, \text{hours}
$$

---

### Final Answer
The time it takes Tom to get back is $\boxed{5}$ hours. $\boxed{5}$

